The desk marked #4471 as one known month/day parsing issue. The merged reports instead
describe **two different causes**. Count distinct report IDs, not export rows: the ticket
system's export pages overlap. Classify by each tenant's locale and booking form, not the
browser-derived intake tag or the report's subject.

## Engineering outcome (checked 2026-10-28)

The live course page now reports #4471 closed on 2026-10-05. The
[parser ticket](https://github.com/Snejki/mega-dev/issues/1) is **fixed**:
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`, closed as completed. The
[hint ticket](https://github.com/Snejki/mega-dev/issues/2) is **won't fix**:
booking-v3 was retired, closed as not planned. Retirement is not a hint fix.
These are reported engineering outcomes, not independent production verification.
The exported October 4 data below describes the historical reports.

## Original problems and separate fixes

1. **Month-first shared parser for day-first tenants (`parser`).** The shared parser reads
   dates as MM/DD even when the tenant writes DD/MM. This is the main cause behind #4471.
   Repair the parser's handling of the tenant's date order.
2. **Misleading date hint in booking-v3 (`hint`).** For day-first tenants, booking-v3 prints
   MM/DD/YYYY under the date field. People enter the date month-first as instructed, but
   the form interprets it in the tenant's day-first order. Correct the displayed hint to
   match the date order that the form actually reads. This needs its own ticket and fix.

The table below computes the number of reports for each cause from the exported data.
Its *tagged as* column shows intake guesses, not the actual cause. Reports described by the
desk as a one-day move remain filed under their tenant-derived cause and are footnoted
there; they do not form a third cause on this evidence.

